bbackup Debian package
=======================

This package installs one self-contained, versioned tree at @VERSION_ROOT@:
Python modules, the locked runtime dependency set, and the verified restic binary.
The stable entry points are /usr/bin/bbackup and /usr/bin/bbman. They invoke
system Python @PYTHON_VERSION@ in isolated mode; they do not install Python
packages into the system interpreter and do not access a package index at runtime.

Configuration, private bindings, credentials, repositories, and backup state are
not package payload. Package removal and upgrades do not remove or roll back that
data. No service is enabled or started by package hooks.

The package also installs disabled `bbackup@.service` and `bbackup@.timer`
templates under `/usr/lib/systemd/system`; each timer instance defaults to
`daily` and does nothing until explicitly enabled. The service runs as root
under the system manager's default configuration. The templates use
`/etc/bbackup/config.json` and `/etc/bbackup/bindings.json`. Installation
creates neither file, enrolls no configuration or credentials, and supplies no
default secrets. The package does not enable or start a timer.
Before activation, provide reviewed
configuration and private bindings, enroll them with the production `setup`
command, and verify them with `doctor` and `jobs list`. For non-daily or
per-job schedules, use the production `service render` command to create
concrete job-specific units; the generic timer is daily only.

Each command holds a shared flock on /run/lock/bbackup-package.lock for its
entire process lifetime. Upgrade and removal hooks take a nonblocking exclusive
lock and refuse while a command is active. They leave a root-owned marker at
/var/lib/bbackup-package/upgrade-pending before changing package files. Launchers
refuse to run while that marker exists. Successful configure verifies every
versioned runtime file, installed systemd templates, tmpfiles rule, and launchers
against the installed checksum manifest before clearing the marker.

If dpkg aborts an upgrade or removal after restoring the previous package, its
installed abort hook verifies the same payload manifest and executables. A real
transition marker requires the exclusive lock before validation and removal.
If refusal happened before a marker was created, the hook instead uses a shared
lock, confirms that no marker appeared, and validates without deleting anything.
This allows already-running clients without admitting a transition writer.
Older installed builds lacking that marker-free abort path can be left
half-configured after an active-client refusal. Stop clients and install the
corrected matching package before relying on the corrected abort behavior.

Do not remove a transition marker manually. Inspect dpkg status and logs; run
`sudo dpkg --configure -a` only if dpkg reports an unconfigured package and the
installed hook can validate its payload. Otherwise reinstall the preserved
matching `.deb` after stopping clients, then inspect `sudo dpkg --audit`.
No backup or restore operation is replayed.

If a transition is refused, stop all bbackup and bbman clients and retry the
package operation. For a failed or interrupted transaction, inspect dpkg's
status and logs, then use the recovery steps above. Package repair does not
replay backup operations. If repair cannot verify the installed files, keep the
package closed and investigate before retrying.

To roll back, install the preserved older .deb using dpkg or apt after stopping
clients. Package rollback changes only program files; it never rolls user data
backward. Keep configuration, credentials, repositories, and state backed up
according to their own retention policy.

The reboot lock file is recreated by systemd-tmpfiles from
/usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/bbackup.conf. If it is unexpectedly missing, the launchers
fail closed; run `sudo systemd-tmpfiles --create /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/bbackup.conf`
or repair the package. Third-party license texts supplied in dependency wheels
are retained with their installed distribution metadata and copied, when present,
to /usr/share/doc/bbackup/third-party-licenses/. See `copyright` for project and
third-party attribution boundaries.
